{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" release, slice 2: the reader's page, built and checked at $0 (2026-11-23)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Result.** Slice 2 is built to `RELEASE-PAGE.md` revision 7, which council 76h found ready after eight rounds (76 to 76h). The\n     4\tpackage now holds the reader's page, a pure function of its data (format `hivemind-release/2`). The $0 screen check of D756 passes:\n     5\t- the stored number question `old-1/v8`, which has a forecast and a history, shows only its question, its sources and \"Forecast\n     6\t  unavailable\";\n     7\t- the two known Yes/No leaks show \"Answer: Yes\" without their explanations;\n     8\t- every negative page is caught for its own reason.\n     9\t\n    10\tNothing is committed, published or deployed. The POC is unchanged.\n    11\t\n    12\t- **Code:** `src/hivemind/release.py`: `page_template`, `render_page`, the format-2 package and its check.\n    13\t- **Tests:** `tests/test_release.py`, tests 26–29.\n    14\t- **Screen check:** `release_screen_prepare.py` and `release_screen.mjs` (in this folder).\n    15\t- **Run:** `poc/data/release-screen-2026-11-23b/`: `screen-expected.json`, sha256 `7934eb2a…`, frozen before the run;\n    16\t  `screen-results.json`; `old-1-v8.png`. The first run, `release-screen-2026-11-23/`, passed on the template before the one change\n    17\t  below and is kept for the record.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. No regressions\n    20\t\n    21\t- **Full suite:** 429 passed. The 40 release tests are part of it, and so are the frozen-ruler guards.\n    22\t- **Mutants:** 37 of 38 caught by failing tests, judged from each failure's full message. The six slice-2 mutants are caught:\n    23\t  - the embed not escaping `<`;\n    24\t  - the quote written with `innerHTML`;\n    25\t  - the source address made a link;\n    26\t  - the package page not compared with its data;\n    27\t  - the page hash unchecked;\n    28\t  - the CSP hash taken over another script.\n    29\t- **The one miss** is the canonical-day mutant, equivalent on Python 3.10, as in slice 1.\n    30\t- **The four-store check under format 2** (`release_check.py`, `poc/data/release-check-2026-11-23i/`) passes every item it names.\n    31\t  Each forged copy now carries the page made from its forged data, so the store comparison is what refuses it.\n    32\t\n    33\t## 2. The screen check (design §6), headless Chrome 158 through its DevTools protocol\n    34\t\n    35\t| Check | Result |\n    36\t|---|---|\n    37\t| `old-1` page, light | renders in 0.05 s (budget 3 s); one request, the page itself; no log error, exception or console error; zero links, `href` or `src`; background `rgb(245, 242, 235)` |\n    38\t| `v8` collapsed | the whole read page equals the frozen page. The card reads \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\", \"Open\", \"Forecast unavailable\", \"1284 pieces of evidence\" |\n    39\t| `v8` expanded, then closed and reopened | the whole read page equals the frozen page with all 1,284 evidence groups, in order, grouped, each source as plain text |\n    40\t| 390 px, dark | no horizontal scroll (390); background `rgb(15, 20, 13)`; the page with `v8` expanded equals the frozen page; Morningstar's quote wraps over 5 lines within the screen (36–374 px) |\n    41\t| `old-3/v36` at 2026-08-25 | \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping Rubin Ultra by December 31, 2027?\", \"Open\", \"Answer: Yes\", \"1 piece of evidence\", and its one entry; nothing else |\n    42\t| `old-1/v10` at 2026-08-28 | the Firmus question, \"Open\", \"Answer: Yes\", \"1 piece of evidence\", and its one entry; nothing else |\n    43\t| Hostile quote (`</script><img onerror>`, `&`, U+2028) | shown exactly as text; no `img` element; no error |\n    44\t| Size | the `old-1` page is 5,794,575 bytes against 5,788,634 of data (budget: data + 64 KB) |\n    45\t\n    46\tEvery read text in these checks also passed the not-clipped and contrast rules: no clipping ancestor, and at least 4.5:1 in light and\n    47\tdark.\n    48\t\n    49\t**The negatives,** each a copy of the `old-1` page with its script changed and its CSP hash recomputed so the changed script runs:\n    50\t\n    51\t| Negative | Caught by |\n    52\t|---|---|\n    53\t| a fetch of `http://127.0.0.1:59999/library` restored | the page rendered, and Chrome logged a `security` error naming the address and `connect-src` |\n    54\t| \"Forecast: $361 billion\" added inside the `v8` card | the whole-page comparison (the card read the extra line) |\n    55\t| the same line added beside the card | the whole-page comparison (the line landed in `other`) |\n    56\t| the list made transparent | no card ever became visible, so the 3 s deadline was missed |\n    57\t| quotes clipped to one line | 1,220 texts clipped at 390 px |\n    58\t| quotes in a one-line `contain: paint` wrapper | 1,284 texts with a clipping ancestor |\n    59\t| the marker drawn dark on its own dark background, in light | all 19 markers below 4.5:1 |\n    60\t| the marker drawn in the page colour, in dark | all 19 markers below 4.5:1 |\n    61\t\n    62\t**Measured, recorded in the design:** Chrome reports a CSP-blocked fetch as two log errors, not as a network request. A scratchpad\n    63\tprobe page named port 8788 once. Chrome refused it under the CSP before connecting, so nothing reached that service.\n    64\t\n    65\t## 3. One change during the build\n    66\t\n    67\t- **What.** The first screenshot showed a dangling comma when a source has no original day (\"original: NVIDIA,\"). The page now adds\n    68\t  \", <day>\" only when there is a day.\n    69\t- **How it was re-certified.** That changed the pinned template, so the expectation was frozen again in a new folder\n    70\t  (`release-screen-2026-11-23b`), the screen check re-run (zero failures, all eight negatives caught), and the new template hash\n    71\t  pinned (`f94bb3cb…`, test 29).\n    72\t\n    73\t## 4. Not shown, and still open\n    74\t\n    75\t- **The idea path in the release** (council 74): deferred, its acceptance pending.\n    76\t- **The evidence-summary disagreement** (`RELEASE-BUILD.md` §8): recorded, with the cheaper course taken.\n    77\t- **Commit, publish and deploy:** Alex's.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" release, slice 2: the reader's page (design for council round 76)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status: revision 7, ready per council 76h; built and screen-checked** (`RELEASE-PAGE-RESULT.md`; build review in council 77).\n     4\tDuring the build, the evidence detail line was changed to add \", <day>\" only when the original day exists. The template was\n     5\tre-certified by a new screen run and its hash re-pinned.\n     6\t\n     7\t**Revision 7 answers `codex-r76g.md`:**\n     8\t- **Contrast starts at the text's own element.** Its background is its own non-transparent `background-color`, or else the nearest\n     9\t  ancestor's. A marker with dark text on its own dark background inside a light card fails.\n    10\t- **The whole template is pinned.** `page_template()` is the complete page text with the CSP filled and one data placeholder;\n    11\t  `render_page` splits it at that placeholder and inserts the escaped data. The test pins the sha256 of `page_template()`, so a\n    12\t  change to any part of the page, the fallback sentence included, fails the pin until re-certified.\n    13\t\n    14\tCouncil 76g judged further checks against hypothetical CSS optional once these are in: a template change already fails the pin.\n    15\t\n    16\t**Revision 6 answers `codex-r76f.md` with general rules instead of one patch per trick** (AGENTS §1: \"certify what the reader can\n    17\tread\" drew a new finding in 76c, 76d and 76f):\n    18\t1. **No clipping ancestor.** No element between a read text and `body` may clip it: computed `overflow-x` or `overflow-y` other than\n    19\t   `visible`, `contain` holding `paint`, `strict` or `content`, or `clip-path` other than `none`.\n    20\t2. **Contrast.** Every read text has a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 between its computed colour and its effective background (the\n    21\t   nearest ancestor with a non-transparent background), in light and in dark. This is WCAG's AA level for body text.\n    22\t3. **The template is pinned.** The sha256 of `PAGE_STYLE` and `PAGE_SCRIPT` are literals in the tests. The screen check certifies\n    23\t   that exact template, and any template change fails the tests until it is re-certified by a new screen run and a new format\n    24\t   version.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Revision 5 answers `codex-r76d.md`:** no text may be clipped.\n    27\t- The template's stylesheet uses none of `overflow`, `clip`, `text-overflow`, `line-clamp`, `max-height` or `nowrap`.\n    28\t- In the browser, every block element holding read text shows all of it. This is checked at desktop width and at 390 px, with the\n    29\t  stored Morningstar entry as the test.\n    30\t- A clipping copy is added as a negative page.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Revision 4 answers `codex-r76c.md`:**\n    33\t- the screen check compares the whole page's visible text, header and every card, with a frozen list; any text outside a known place\n    34\t  fails;\n    35\t- visibility is one test, `checkVisibility` with opacity and visibility, for the evidence and for the timer;\n    36\t- the template's stylesheet draws no text (no `content`);\n    37\t- a sibling forecast line and a fully transparent list are added as negative pages.\n    38\t\n    39\t**Revision 3 answers `codex-r76b.md`:**\n    40\t- the screen check compares every visible text of each checked card with a frozen list, in order and grouped by evidence, so an\n    41\t  extra line fails;\n    42\t- each dated card is frozen from its own dated package;\n    43\t- the timer stops when the list is visible;\n    44\t- the dark background is the literal `rgb(15, 20, 13)`.\n    45\t\n    46\tRevision 2 answered `codex-r76.md`:\n    47\t- source addresses are plain text, never links;\n    48\t- the escaping is written out as literal JSON escapes, with the charset and the CSP first in the file;\n    49\t- the format-1 checks are updated, and a lone page-hash error has its own test;\n    50\t- the screen check compares the expanded `v8` card with frozen expected entries;\n    51\t- the fetch mutant keeps its script running and must name `connect-src` and its destination;\n    52\t- the fallback, timer, phone-width and dark-scheme conditions are made observable;\n    53\t- the text filter is dropped. Slice 1 is built and accepted (D759: `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11,\n    54\t`RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`). Council 73 (D758) ordered the exporter first and the reader's screen second. This slice completes D756's\n    55\tscreen check: a $0 check of the reader's screen on a stored number question that has a forecast and history, where only the\n    56\tquestion, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear.\n    57\t\n    58\t## 0. Scope\n    59\t\n    60\t- **Built:**\n    61\t  - a page that is a pure function of the package data;\n    62\t  - the package gains that page (format 2);\n    63\t  - the package check proves the page equals that function of the data;\n    64\t  - a headless-browser check of the `old-1/v8` screen.\n    65\t- **The page reads only the package's data,** embedded in it.\n    66\t  - No network request of any kind: no live POC service, no `/library` fetch, no translated or prepared JSON, no composite trials,\n    67\t    no fonts or images from outside.\n    68\t  - No idea submission control or route (council 74).\n    69\t- **Not in scope:**\n    70\t  - the POC's page, API and MCP, unchanged;\n    71\t  - colleagues' page;\n    72\t  - the idea path;\n    73\t  - any model call;\n    74\t  - commit, publish, deploy (Alex's).\n    75\t\n    76\t**Why a separate page, not the POC's library UI.** The POC's components read the `thesis-ui/2` contract, which needs a ladder,\n    77\tscores and judgements for every thesis; a number question in the package has none. Adapting those shared components would change\n    78\tthe POC, which stays unchanged (design §2, D757). A small page written for the package has nothing to adapt and nothing that can\n    79\treach the network.\n    80\t\n    81\t## 1. What is built\n    82\t\n    83\t**A visible fallback.** The page starts with the line \"This release page needs JavaScript to show its questions.\" The script removes it\n    84\tonce the list is built. A browser that blocks the inline script keeps that line (`<noscript>` would stay hidden there; council\n    85\t76).\n    86\t\n    87\t**In `src/hivemind/release.py`:**\n    88\t- **`FORMAT = 'hivemind-release/2'`.** The data's allow-list (`release-allow-list/1`) is unchanged.\n    89\t- **`PACKAGE_FILES = {'manifest.json', 'release-data.json', 'index.html'}`.** The manifest's closed set gains `page_sha256`.\n    90\t- **`render_page(data_bytes) -> bytes`:** one fixed template, the module constant `PAGE_TEMPLATE`, with the data inserted once.\n    91\t  - **The data** goes into `<script type=\"application/json\" id=\"release-data\">`, written from the exact `release-data.json` bytes,\n    92\t    with each of these characters replaced by its six-character JSON escape: `<` by `\\u003c`, `>` by `\\u003e`, `&` by `\\u0026`,\n    93\t    U+2028 by `\\u2028`, U+2029 by `\\u2029`. These replacements stay valid JSON and parse back to the same data. The block can then\n    94\t    hold no `</script` and no `<!--`.\n    95\t  - **The file starts** with `<!doctype html>`, then `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then the CSP `<meta>`, before any style, script or\n    96\t    data.\n    97\t  - **A Content-Security-Policy `<meta>`:**\n    98\t    `default-src 'none'; script-src 'sha256-<inline script>'; style-src 'sha256-<inline style>'; img-src 'none'; font-src 'none';\n    99\t    connect-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'`. The two hashes are computed from the template's own script and style,\n   100\t    so the browser refuses any other script, style or connection.\n   101\t  - **The script** builds the page only with `document.createElement`, `textContent` and `className`. It creates no `<a>` element,\n   102\t    no `href` and no `src`. It never uses `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write`, `eval`, `Function`, or any network API\n   103\t    (`fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket`, `EventSource`, `navigator.sendBeacon`, `import()`, `Worker`).\n   104\t  - **The style** uses system fonts and inline colour tokens copied from `web/design-system/tokens.css`. It generates no text: no\n   105\t    `content` property anywhere, so every word a reader sees is a text node the screen check can read. It clips no text: none of\n   106\t    `overflow`, `clip`, `text-overflow`, `line-clamp`, `max-height` or `white-space: nowrap`, and long words wrap\n   107\t    (`overflow-wrap: anywhere`). It has a dark scheme under\n   108\t    `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, and it works at phone width.\n   109\t- **`write_package`** also writes `index.html = render_page(data bytes)` and records its sha256.\n   110\t- **`check_package`** also:\n   111\t  - reads `index.html` once through the same folder handle;\n   112\t  - requires its bytes to equal `render_page(the data bytes it read)` exactly;\n   113\t  - requires the manifest's `page_sha256` to match.\n   114\t\n   115\t  A page that differs by one byte from the function of the data is refused. So a page cannot carry anything the data does not hold,\n   116\t  and no code the template does not hold.\n   117\t\n   118\t**What the page shows,** in package order. Each shown value sits in an element with a fixed class, so the screen check can read it:\n   119\t`question-text`, `question-status`, `question-forecast`, `question-answer`, `evidence-count`, `evidence-summary`, `evidence-quote`,\n   120\t`evidence-meta`, `source-title`, `source-publisher`, `source-day`, `source-address`.\n   121\t- **A header:** \"Hivemind release\", the package's day, and the count of questions by class.\n   122\t- **Each question:**\n   123\t  - its question text and \"Open\" or \"Closed\";\n   124\t  - for a number question, the marker \"Forecast unavailable\";\n   125\t  - for an event or Yes/No question, \"Answer: <label>\", or \"Not yet answered\" when the answer is null;\n   126\t  - \"<n> pieces of evidence\", and a `<details>` element. When opened, it builds the evidence list:\n   127\t    - each piece shows its summary, the quote, and a line with tier, speaker, and original speaker and day;\n   128\t    - each source shows title, publisher, day and its address as plain text. The page has no links, so neither a click nor a script\n   129\t      can make a request (council 76).\n   130\t\n   131\t  Evidence is built only when opened, so the first screen stays fast on a 6 MB package.\n   132\t\n   133\t## 2. State × event (the page and the check)\n   134\t\n   135\t| State / event | What happens |\n   136\t|---|---|\n   137\t| Package with no questions | \"No questions as of <day>\" |\n   138\t| A question with no evidence | \"No evidence in force\", and no `<details>` |\n   139\t| A source address of any scheme, `javascript:` included | shown as text; the page has no links |\n   140\t| Text holding `</script>`, `<img onerror=…>`, `&` or U+2028 | the embed escapes it, the page shows it as text, and the JSON parses back identical |\n   141\t| The page is opened from disk, a static host or any path | it reads only its embedded data; nothing else exists to read |\n   142\t| The browser blocks the inline script | the fallback line stays visible; nothing else renders, and the page holds no forecast to leak |\n   143\t| A page edited after the build (a fetch added, a field added) | `check_package` refuses: the bytes differ from `render_page(data)` |\n   144\t| The template changes in a later version | old packages fail `check_package` under the new code by design; a new template is a new format version |\n   145\t| A dark scheme and a phone width | checked in §6: at 390 px wide the page has no horizontal scroll; with the dark scheme emulated, the body's background is the dark token |\n   146\t\n   147\t## 3. Value × reader\n   148\t\n   149\t- Every value the page shows comes from the embedded data; the page reads no other value.\n   150\t- The page's readers are a person's browser and the browser check.\n   151\t- The data's readers are unchanged from slice 1: `check_package`, `check_origins`, the tests. The page adds one reader of the data,\n   152\t  the page script, and no new writer.\n   153\t\n   154\t## 4. New path × old checks\n   155\t\n   156\t| Slice-1 check | Slice 2 |\n   157\t|---|---|\n   158\t| Closed set of the data, origin check, staged check before the no-replace publication, the no-replace report | unchanged |\n   159\t| The manifest's closed set | gains `page_sha256`; `format` becomes `hivemind-release/2` |\n   160\t| Exactly the package files, each read once, no links, no pipes | the same rule over three files |\n   161\t| The literal-values rule of verification (council 75d) | applied to the page checks as well |\n   162\t| `release_check.py` pins `hivemind-release/1`; its helpers that forge data refresh only the data hash | the pin becomes `/2`. The forging helpers also regenerate `index.html = render_page(forged data)` and its hash, so a forged forecast still reaches the store comparison and is refused there, with its exact reason |\n   163\t\n   164\t## 5. Tests (`tests/test_release.py`) and mutants\n   165\t\n   166\t1. **Determinism.** `render_page` of the fixture data twice gives the same bytes.\n   167\t2. **Escaping.**\n   168\t   - A fixture quote holding `</script><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`, an `&` and a U+2028 is embedded with the literal escapes.\n   169\t   - The page bytes hold no `</script` inside the data block and no `<!--`.\n   170\t   - Parsing the block back gives the data unchanged.\n   171\t   - In the browser check (§6), on the fixture page with that evidence expanded, the exact quote is visible as text, and the page has\n   172\t     no `img` element.\n   173\t3. **No network code, no drawn text.**\n   174\t   - The template's script contains none of the listed APIs, and no `createElement('a')`, `href` or `src`.\n   175\t   - Its CSP is exactly the policy above, with the hashes of its own script and style.\n   176\t   - Its stylesheet has no `content` property, and none of `overflow` (as a property name, so `overflow-wrap` is allowed), `clip`,\n   177\t     `clip-path`, `contain`, `text-overflow`, `line-clamp`, `max-height`, `nowrap`, `opacity` or `visibility`.\n   178\t   - The sha256 of `page_template()` (the whole page with its one data placeholder) equals a literal written in the test after the\n   179\t     screen run certifies it. A change to any part of the page fails this until it is re-certified.\n   180\t4. **The package check:**\n   181\t   - a page changed by one byte, with the manifest hash refreshed, is refused with its exact reason;\n   182\t   - a page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added, its CSP hash and manifest hash both updated, is refused, because it\n   183\t     is not `render_page(data)`;\n   184\t   - **a correct page with only `page_sha256` wrong is refused with its exact reason**, which catches a deleted hash check;\n   185\t   - a missing page and a fourth file are each refused;\n   186\t   - all three files are read once through the folder handle, and the page bytes compared are the bytes hashed.\n   187\t5. **Literal identifiers:** `hivemind-release/2` and `release-allow-list/1`, in the tests and the acceptance script.\n   188\t\n   189\t**Mutants:**\n   190\t- `render_page` not escaping `<`;\n   191\t- the script using `innerHTML` for the quote;\n   192\t- a source address rendered as a link;\n   193\t- `check_package` not comparing the page with `render_page`;\n   194\t- `check_package` not checking `page_sha256`;\n   195\t- the CSP hash computed over a different script.\n   196\t\n   197\t## 6. The $0 screen check (`release_screen.mjs`, run once)\n   198\t\n   199\t- **Setup.** Headless Chrome from `/Applications/Google Chrome.app`, driven through its DevTools protocol with Node's built-in\n   200\t  WebSocket, on a fresh profile in the scratchpad. Each page is opened from `file://`.\n   201\t- **Packages:** `old-1` at its default day, and the two dated leak packages (`old-3` at 2026-08-25, `old-1` at 2026-08-28), built by\n   202\t  the slice-1 command under format 2.\n   203\t\n   204\t**Recorded for each page:**\n   205\t- the page's own network requests (`Network.requestWillBeSent` for the page target). Only the document itself may appear;\n   206\t- console errors, exceptions and CSP violations (`Runtime.exceptionThrown`, `Log.entryAdded`). Zero are allowed.\n   207\t\n   208\t**Observation.** The `Network`, `Log` and `Runtime` domains are enabled before navigation and stay enabled through every expansion.\n   209\tAny of these fails the page:\n   210\t- a request other than the document;\n   211\t- a `Log.entryAdded` error;\n   212\t- a `Runtime.exceptionThrown`;\n   213\t- a `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` of type `error`.\n   214\t\n   215\t**Expected values, frozen before the run.** `release_screen.mjs` reads `screen-expected.json`. It is written before the first screen\n   216\trun, with its sha256 recorded in the result, from the accepted slice-1 packages:\n   217\t- `poc/data/release-check-2026-11-23g/old-1/package` for `v8`;\n   218\t- `old-3/package-2026-08-25` for `old-3/v36` (at that day it asks whether NVIDIA will begin shipping Rubin Ultra);\n   219\t- `old-1/package-2026-08-28` for `old-1/v10`.\n   220\t\n   221\tFor each checked page it holds the page's complete visible text:\n   222\t- **the header:** \"Hivemind release\", \"As of <day>\", and the counts line;\n   223\t- **every card collapsed,** in package order: its id, then its question, status, the marker or the answer, and the evidence count\n   224\t  (or \"No evidence in force\");\n   225\t- **the checked card expanded:** for each evidence entry in order, its own group: summary, quote, meta line, and per source its\n   226\t  title, publisher, day and address.\n   227\t\n   228\t**How the page is read.**\n   229\t- **What counts as visible.** The check walks every text node of `document.body` in document order. It keeps a text only when its\n   230\t  element passes `checkVisibility({opacityProperty: true, visibilityProperty: true, contentVisibilityAuto: true})` and has a\n   231\t  non-zero box. A text inside a transparent, hidden or collapsed ancestor is not visible.\n   232\t- **Not clipped.**\n   233\t  - For each kept text whose element is a block (not `display: inline`), the element's `scrollWidth` is at most `clientWidth + 1` and\n   234\t    its `scrollHeight` at most `clientHeight + 1`.\n   235\t  - No ancestor between it and `body` clips: none has `overflow-x` or `overflow-y` other than `visible`, `contain` holding `paint`,\n   236\t    `strict` or `content`, or `clip-path` other than `none`.\n   237\t  - Any clipped text fails the page, whatever its content.\n   238\t- **Readable.** Each kept text's contrast ratio is at least 4.5:1, computed (WCAG relative luminance) between its element's computed\n   239\t  `color` and the first non-transparent `background-color` found from that element itself upward, the page canvas if none. This is checked in light and in\n   240\t  dark. Any text below the ratio fails the page.\n   241\t- **Where each text goes:** to the header, to a card (by `data-id`), to an evidence group within a card, or else to `other`.\n   242\t  `other` must be empty.\n   243\t- **What must match.** The read page must equal the frozen page exactly: collapsed, then with the checked card expanded, then after\n   244\t  closing and reopening it.\n   245\t- **What that catches.** An extra visible line anywhere fails: inside the card, beside it, or in the header. For example,\n   246\t  \"Forecast: $361 billion\", Morningstar's genuine figure shown as if it were the system's. So does a missing, duplicated, regrouped\n   247\t  or reordered entry.\n   248\t\n   249\t**The screen of `old-1/v8`:**\n   250\t- **Collapsed:** the whole read page equals the frozen page. The `v8` card reads \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA\n   251\t  report for FY2027?\", \"Open\", \"Forecast unavailable\", \"1284 pieces of evidence\".\n   252\t- **With `v8` expanded:** the whole read page equals the frozen page with `v8`'s 1,284 groups. The full Morningstar quote and its\n   253\t  attribution are included, and \"Bullish\" appears only where a frozen source text holds it.\n   254\t- **Closed and reopened once:** the same.\n   255\t- **No link anywhere on the page:** zero `a` elements, zero `[href]`, zero `[src]`.\n   256\t- **A screenshot** of the expanded card's top is saved with the result.\n   257\t\n   258\t**The leak screens.** For `old-3/v36` at 2026-08-25 and `old-1/v10` at 2026-08-28:\n   259\t- the page's day equals its package's;\n   260\t- the whole read page, collapsed and with the card expanded, equals the frozen page from that dated package. The card holds its\n   261\t  question, status, \"Answer: Yes\", \"1 piece of evidence\", then its single evidence entry and source;\n   262\t- so the entry is visible, and nothing else is: neither judgement's explanation, nor \"around $1 trillion\", \"rung 4\" or \"at rung\".\n   263\t\n   264\t**The decisive negatives** (checks of the check). Each is a copy of the `old-1` page with its script changed and its CSP hash\n   265\trecomputed, so the changed script runs. Each must fail for its own reason:\n   266\t- **inside the card:** one visible line \"Forecast: $361 billion\" added in the `v8` card fails the comparison;\n   267\t- **beside the card:** the same line added just before the `v8` card, as its sibling, fails the comparison (it lands in `other`);\n   268\t- **transparent:** the whole list set to `opacity: 0` misses the 3 s deadline, because no card is visible, although every card\n   269\t  exists;\n   270\t- **clipped:** quotes held to one line (`white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis` added through the script's\n   271\t  inline style on each quote) fail the not-clipped rule at 390 px, although their text is identical;\n   272\t- **clipping wrapper:** each quote placed inside a one-line wrapper (`contain: paint; height: 1.5em`), the quote itself wrapping\n   273\t  normally, fails the clipping-ancestor rule at 390 px;\n   274\t- **unreadable marker:** in dark, the marker drawn in `--ink` on an `--ink` background (`color` set to `rgb(15, 20, 13)` through the\n   275\t  script's inline style) fails the contrast rule, although its text, box and opacity pass;\n   276\t- **marker on its own background:** in light, the marker given dark text on its own dark background (`color` and `background` both\n   277\t  `rgb(15, 20, 13)`) inside the light card fails the contrast rule.\n   278\t\n   279\t**The escaping screen.** The fixture page of test 2, with that evidence expanded, shows the exact quote as text and has no `img`\n   280\telement.\n   281\t\n   282\t**The fetch mutant.**\n   283\t- **Setup:** a copy of the `old-1` page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added to its script, and the CSP script hash\n   284\t  recomputed for the altered script, so the script runs.\n   285\t- **It must fail the check for the right reason:**\n   286\t  - the normal list renders;\n   287\t  - a `Log.entryAdded` security error names `http://127.0.0.1:59999/library` and `connect-src`.\n   288\t\n   289\t  A page whose script is blocked wholesale does not earn the credit.\n   290\t- **Measured on this Mac (Chrome 158, headless, scratchpad probe):** a CSP-blocked fetch produces no `Network.requestWillBeSent`. It\n   291\t  produces a `security` error naming the address and the directive, and a `javascript` error.\n   292\t- **The probe page named port 8788.** Chrome refused it under the CSP before connecting (\"Refused to connect\"), so no request reached\n   293\t  that service. Port 59999 is not on the fetch specification's blocked-port list, and nothing listens there.\n   294\t\n   295\t**Phone width and dark scheme.**\n   296\t- **At 390 × 844** (`Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride`):\n   297\t  - `document.scrollingElement.scrollWidth` is at most 390;\n   298\t  - with `v8` expanded, the whole read page, with the not-clipped rule, equals the frozen expanded page;\n   299\t  - Morningstar's quote (`e2295`), scrolled into view, wraps over more than one line (`getClientRects` on its text), and its box lies\n   300\t    within the 390 px viewport.\n   301\t- With `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, the body's computed background is exactly `rgb(15, 20, 13)` (the\n   302\t  shared `--ink`, used as the dark page colour). In light, it is `rgb(245, 242, 235)` (`--paper`).\n   303\t\n   304\t**Budget,** set before the first control:\n   305\t- at most 3 s for the `old-1` page in headless Chrome, from `Page.navigate` until the full list is visible: the fallback line is\n   306\t  gone, and every expected question's card is present and passes the same visibility test as the text;\n   307\t- the page's size at most the data's bytes plus 64 KB.\n   308\t\n   309\t## 7. Prior decisions\n   310\t\n   311\tD726, D756, D757, D758, D759; council 74 (no idea input); council 75d (literal expected values); D690 (poc-v118 stays the product);\n   312\tD163 (English product text).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '101,220p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -n 3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md | cut -c1-1000; tail -n 10 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | cut -c1-1000\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '101,220p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -n 3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md | cut -c1-1000; tail -n 10 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | cut -c1-1000\"","aggregated_output":"     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n  reasons a theme cited as a dependency (PRD 11, E4), and its stored path holds the 2026-07-05 fault above (frozen; findings for\n  Alex); the same matter takes different shapes between draws (Kyber due end-2027 in one draw and end-2028 in another); under D397\n  the second bet of a topic that holds two has no answer; five-state event ladders failed by hand three times before a construction now in the request\n  produced ones that pass a reader; in Stage A the model's own are faulted by both reader families in 10 of 17 for the company.\n- **The unseeded measurement (D395):** graded subset 4.0 against 6.33 seeded. Every miss is a question not opened in the ruler's shape.\n  Judged answers 16 of 16, on the ruler's wording and ladder; the system's own questions have never met a judge.\n- **Stage 2 of D392:** clean 18, 18, 17 against v108 17, 15, 16, inconclusive on q12.\n- **Known gap (D394):** capital spending (q12).\n\nNo long job is running.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
